# 21-OPS / team and management

**Goes here.** Who does the work, and how the work gets run. Hiring and first hires
by stage, roles and ownership, compensation and incentives, SOP writing and
documentation, delegation and founder time, in-house versus agency versus
freelancer, managing agencies and contractors, weekly operating rhythm and project
management, the team tooling stack, customer service staffing and SLAs, and
fulfilment operations including 3PL selection, pick and pack, carriers and warehouse
SLAs.

**Why this module exists.** Same argument as FIN, on a different constraint. The OS
produces recommendations, but somebody has to execute them. **Capacity is a
precondition on plays, not a footnote.** A brand with 1 founder and no designer
cannot execute a 40-creative-a-month plan, and a system that recommends it anyway is
producing a to-do list nobody will ever action. Cash is the FIN constraint. People
and time are the OPS constraint. Both gate what the system is allowed to suggest.

## The test that matters here

**Does the source contain a threshold with a number attached?**

- At what order volume does a brand need a second person?
- At what ticket volume does support need a dedicated hire?
- What does a 3PL cost per order at 500, 5,000 and 50,000 units a month?
- At what revenue does the founder stop buying media themselves?

Those thresholds are what the play preconditions need, and they are a handful of
cards rather than a library. Collect them out of phase order, before anything else
in this folder.

If a source has no number of that kind, it almost certainly fails the discard test.

## Skip, and this folder attracts these badly

**General leadership and management books.** Multipliers, The Five Dysfunctions of a
Team, Good to Great, and the entire Wharton and HBR genre. They are good books and
they are not ecommerce operating knowledge. A frontier model already states their
content unprompted, which is the discard test failing. 4 such files are currently in
this folder and should come out.

Also skip: big company org design, productivity advice, and culture content with no
operating mechanism attached.

**The line.** "Build psychological safety in your team" is a book. "At 40 tickets a
day you need a dedicated support hire, and below that a shared inbox with 3 macros
beats hiring" is a card.

## Not here

- What to say to an upset customer, and the recovery policy -> 17-CXP
- Supplier and factory negotiation -> 18-COG
- Whether the business can afford a hire, and how it flows through the P&L -> 20-FIN
- Purchase order planning and inventory financing -> 20-FIN
- What the ads actually say -> 10-MCR and 11-ADC
- Store configuration click paths -> 05-SET
- Positioning and naming -> 04-BRD

3 boundaries worth memorising. **CXP is the content of the customer interaction. OPS
is the desk that handles it**: staffing, macros, SLAs, tooling, escalation.
**COG negotiates the supplier. OPS negotiates the 3PL.** **OPS says when to make a
hire and what it should cost. FIN says whether you can afford it.**

